Amos told the class about an ongoing program of research at the University of Michigan that sought to answer this question: Are people good intuitive statisticians?
We already knew that people are good intuitive grammarians: at age four a child effortlessly conforms to the rules of grammar as she speaks, although she has no idea that such rules exist.
 Do people have a similar intuitive feel for the basic principles of statistics?
Amos reported that the answer was a qualified yes.
 We had a lively debate in the seminar and ultimately concluded that a qualified no was a better answer.
